From 15d31468fa4ec44b121e320bef13da96f92224db Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Alexis Williams <alexis@typedr.at>
Date: Wed, 30 Sep 2026 23:37:37 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 16/16] tests: Build and run GLib's tests on WebAssembly

Only test sources and test build files change.

The tests' meson files treat every non-Windows host as Unix, while the
library build leaves most Unix sources out on Emscripten, so
configuring with -Dtests=true failed and several tests didn't link.
Leave out what Emscripten can't build or run: the Unix-only test blocks
and programs in glib/tests and gio/tests, GIO_LAUNCH_DESKTOP and the
dbus-launch helper; the constructor test, whose shared libraries Meson
can't link here; tests/lib's glibconfig.py, which only girepository's
tests read; and appmonitor (GDesktopAppInfo), gsubprocess (spawning)
and unix-fd (fd passing). The tool tests' runner looks for <tool>.js and
runs it with the Node that _G_TEST_PROGRAM_RUNNER_NODE names, which the
meson files set on Emscripten, glib/tests' included.

WebAssembly traps on any call through a function pointer of another
type, so test code that registers callbacks with the wrong signature
now passes functions of the right type: class, instance and interface
init functions take their data argument, notify and signal handlers
their user data and the signal's parameters, class closures made with
g_cclosure_new() the user data their marshallers pass, and small
wrappers stand in where a test passed a library function (g_strdup,
g_free, g_usleep, g_main_loop_quit, g_cancellable_cancel,
g_object_unref) or one of its own functions through another callback
type. hook's hooks swap functions between g_hook_list_invoke() and
g_hook_list_invoke_check(), which call them through different types;
filenamecompleter connected g_main_loop_quit() to a signal with another
signature. param's instance_init takes the class data argument
GParamSpecTypeInfo gives it here.

Where an expectation can't hold on WebAssembly, skip the subtest, or
the part of it, with the reason:
- musl takes any locale name but has no locale data: collate's lists
  that sort differently under en_US, option-context's decimal comma,
  date's Polish month names, and gdatetime's en_GB formats, de_DE month
  names and eras.
- WasmFS has no sockets, and Emscripten can't spawn or fork processes,
  parse an IPv6 scope ID or list network interfaces; nothing can send it
  a Unix signal. thread's CPU affinity subtest calls
  pthread_getaffinity_np(), which Emscripten declares but doesn't
  define.
- WasmFS keeps file times as a double count of milliseconds and gives
  pipes no capacity.
- Time zones come from Intl, so no TZif file is read by path.
- gio-tool's launch command needs desktop applications and spawning,
  and WebAssembly's content types know no MIME types.

Where musl's output is right but differs, expect it: strftime() pads
years to four digits and has no %k, %l or %P, and iconv() reports a
character the target lacks once it has consumed the input. A
WebAssembly GMainContext polls no wakeup fd unless it is ownerless, so
mainloop expects one fewer fd. The program is named after the .js
script Node runs. gwakeuptest checks that the wakeup's poll fd agrees
with g_wakeup_wait(), and waits with g_wakeup_wait(), since its threaded
test passes tokens far too often for the 10 ms slices a polling wait
takes.

Fix the tests themselves where they relied on more than they should:
- filenamecompleter expected completions in the order one file system
  lists them;
- gdbus-peer's credentials test assumed credentials on every Unix;
- messages-low-memory's malloc() looks up the real one with
  dlsym(RTLD_NEXT), which WebAssembly can't do; there it calls
  emscripten_builtin_malloc() instead;
- the Python tests expected abort() to end Node with SIGABRT;
- spawn-singlethread and test-printf open their pipe with pipe(), and
  test-printf's reader waits for its writer, since Emscripten's pipe
  reads never block;
- asyncqueue's threads test pushed its items as soon as it had created
  its consumers, and expected each consumer to get some. Under load a
  thread can start hundreds of milliseconds after it is created, once
  the queue has drained, so the test now waits until every consumer is
  running. This race is the same on every platform.

convert and converter-stream check that a replaced character is found
also when the conversion runs out of output room or ends in an
incomplete character, and check stateful charsets: a byte order mark and
ISO-2022-JP's shift state across calls and full output, and a fallback
between kanji in ISO-2022-JP.

Upstream-Status: Inappropriate [Wasm-specific]
